on (including swaps). At December 31, 2025, the estimated duration of the investment securities portfolio, which measures price sensitivity to interest rate changes, was 3.8 years, compared with 3.4 years at December 31, 2024, reflecting slower realized prepayment assumptions than previously modeled.","section":"RESULTS OF OPERATIONS","block":723,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000109380-26-000046","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-24","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000109380","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/109380/000010938026000046/zions-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/22aae41b9acb6dfd2ab9ebc02ed49bf8428a388202d41da483599606f94f248e.bin","sha256":"22aae41b9acb6dfd2ab9ebc02ed49bf8428a388202d41da483599606f94f248e"}},{"id":"f23ca694a93399b6536af563ccc90ef47f592aa398085ff58300e45e946ffb9a","quote":"The SBIC investment portfolio increased $67 million, largely driven by new investments and valuation adjustments on related investments. The reduction in FHLB stock resulted from lower FHLB borrowings. To maintain borrowing capacity, we are required to hold FHLB stock equal to approximately 4-5% of our outstanding FHLB borrowings.","context":"Other noninterest-bearing investments increased $56 million, or 5%, during 2025, This growth was primarily attributable to higher balances within our SBIC investment portfolio, partially offset by reductions in holdings of FHLB and Federal Reserve stock.","section":"RESULTS OF OPERATIONS","block":767,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000109380-26-000046","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-24","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000109380","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/109380/000010938026000046/zions-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/22aae41b9acb6dfd2ab9ebc02ed49bf8428a388202d41da483599606f94f248e.bin","sha256":"22aae41b9acb6dfd2ab9ebc02ed49bf8428a388202d41da483599606f94f248e"}},{"id":"e51b61d1a3c287c19b4112ec20f9c9c46c2cf10244b90ed68fa7ba2bf6907cb4","quote":"The FHLB and FRB remain important sources of contingent liquidity and funding. As a member of the FHLB of Des Moines, we have the ability to borrow against eligible loans and securities to meet liquidity and funding needs. To preserve this borrowing capacity, we are required to maintain investments in both FHLB and FRB stock. At December 31, 2025, our total investment in FHLB and FRB stock was $100 million and $54 million, respectively, compared with $124 million and $65 million at December 31, 2024. The average FHLB activity stock holdings in 2025 were $183 million, compared with $85 million in 2024, contributing to an increase in dividends on FHLB activity stock during the year.","context":"In 2025, the primary sources of cash included a decrease in investment securities, net cash provided by operating activities, a decrease in money market investments, and proceeds from the issuance of long-term debt. The primary uses of cash during the same period included an increase in loans and leases, a decrease in brokered deposits, and a decrease in short-term borrowings. Cash payments for interest, reflected in operating expenses, totaled $1.6 billion and $1.9 billion during 2025 and 2024, respectively.","section":"RESULTS OF OPERATIONS","block":1040,"table":null,"attribution":"issuer_disclosure","interpretation":"source_excerpt_not_independently_verified","source":{"accession":"0000109380-26-000046","form":"10-K","filedAt":"2026-02-24","reportDate":"2025-12-31","cik":"0000109380","url":"https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/109380/000010938026000046/zions-20251231.htm","rawPath":"sec/raw/22aae41b9acb6dfd2ab9ebc02ed49bf8428a388202d41da483599606f94f248e.bin","sha256":"22aae41b9acb6dfd2ab9ebc02ed49bf8428a388202d41da483599606f94f248e"}},{"id":"87c939d5a7c3be1fa495a7befacd1d09d68c4b23c740c8ff17a28d08ecb0d79b","quote":"| | | | 